  "image_name": "160dd12aa6d767698a95f16e615c569a.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure places several neuromorphic computing approaches along a spectrum from electronic to photonic implementations. The left side shows conventional neuromorphic electronics: pulse-like signals from multiple pre-neurons pass through weighted connections in a crossbar-style array, and a post-neuron receives the summed weighted inputs. This panel emphasizes the familiar multiply-and-accumulate operation used in neural hardware.\n\nThe middle panels show optical accelerators as intermediate approaches. In the incoherent optical case, separate colored channels carry signals that are independently weighted and then multiplexed together, suggesting parallel processing through wavelength or channel encoding. In the coherent optical case, several weighted optical inputs are combined into one output, highlighting optical interference or field-level combination rather than simple intensity addition.\n\nThe rightmost panel moves furthest into photonics, depicting a neuron-like optical element with several weighted inputs converging on a central node. There, signals are summed and passed through a nonlinear or threshold-like response, producing an output related to a transfer function. Overall, the figure compares how neural-style computation can shift from electronic weighted summation to increasingly optical, neuron-inspired processing, with photonic neurons representing the most fully light-based architecture.",
